description:
Locations (5 sibling MemoryBackend impls, identical pattern):
- `crate-jmap-chat-server/src/memory.rs:535-552`
- `crate-jmap-calendars-server/src/memory.rs:794-820`
- `crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/memory.rs:718-744`
- `crate-jmap-contacts-server/src/memory.rs:713-739`
- `crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:524-550`

  fn json_merge_patch(target: &mut serde_json::Value, patch: serde_json::Value) {
      match patch {
          serde_json::Value::Object(patch_map) => {
              let target_map = target
                  .as_object_mut()
                  .expect("merge patch target must be an object");
              for (key, patch_val) in patch_map {
                  if patch_val.is_null() {
                      target_map.remove(&key);
                  } else {
                      let entry = target_map.entry(key).or_insert(serde_json::Value::Null);
                      json_merge_patch(entry, patch_val);   // <-- direct recursion, no depth bound
                  }
              }
          }
          other => *target = other,
      }
  }

CONCERN
This is the RFC 7396 JSON Merge Patch implementation called by every extension server's `/set update` handler. It recurses directly on the patch tree with no depth bound.

A client-supplied `PatchObject` deserializes into `Map<String, Value>`. The Map keys are JMAP property paths (slash-delimited); the Values are arbitrary JSON that the merge-patch walks recursively. Crucially: `serde_json::from_value::<PatchObject>` only enforces serde_json's default recursion limit on the OUTER Map structure — the inner `Value` leaves are stored as already-parsed `Value` trees and skip the depth check.

An attacker posts an `Email/set update` (or any sibling's `/set update`) with a patch like:
  { "someField": { "a": { "a": { "a": ... 4096 deep ... }}}}

This passes serde_json's outer parse. `json_merge_patch` then recurses 4096 levels, crashing the tokio worker on stack overflow.

The five impls are byte-identical (cookie-cutter from the canonical pattern — though mail-server itself does not use this helper because it works with typed Patch types instead of PatchObject<O>). This is exactly the cookie-cutter divergence pattern that AGENTS.md 'Canonical Templates' rule was added to prevent: fix one, propagate to all.

The `.expect("merge patch target must be an object")` on line 540 is also a latent panic, separately flagged in JMAP-sc1b.87 — that bead resolved 'unreachable on internal callers' but the recursion-bound issue is orthogonal.

WHAT GOES WRONG
DoS (stack overflow). Crashes the tokio worker for any authenticated `/set update` call with a deeply nested patch value.

PROPOSED FIX
Two options:

(a) **Depth cap** in `json_merge_patch` — add a depth parameter, increment on each recursion, return early (without mutation) if it exceeds a constant like 32:

       fn json_merge_patch(target: &mut Value, patch: Value, depth: usize) {
           if depth > MAX_MERGE_DEPTH { return; }  // or: return Err(JmapError::limit("patchDepth"));
           ...
           json_merge_patch(entry, patch_val, depth + 1);
       }

(b) **Iterative rewrite** using an explicit worklist. More invasive but removes the entire stack-DoS class.

Per the canonical-templates rule, apply the fix to ALL FIVE sibling impls in the same commit. If the canonical pattern needs to live somewhere shared, consider lifting `json_merge_patch` into `jmap-server` as a shared helper.

Add a unit test with a 1000-deep patch Value and confirm the depth cap (or rewrite) handles it.

ORACLE
- RFC 7396 §1: 'The merge patch format is a JSON value that represents the modifications.' No depth bound is specified; that is the implementor's responsibility.
- Companion to JMAP-sc1b.95 (json_pointer_ext recursion) and JMAP-sc1b.94 (parse_request doc gap) — three related unbounded-recursion findings.

RISK CLASSIFICATION
P2. Authenticated stack-DoS in every extension-server memory backend. Cookie-cutter pattern means a single fix propagates to all five sites.
